WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.280
All right.

00:04.320 --> 00:07.280
So now we have two tables table A and table B.

00:07.320 --> 00:11.160
And the big question here is how to combine those two tables.

00:11.160 --> 00:12.480
What do we want exactly.

00:12.520 --> 00:15.760
Do you want to combine the rows or the columns.

00:15.800 --> 00:20.960
And now if you say I would like to combine the columns then we are talking about joining tables.

00:20.960 --> 00:23.080
So we're going to use joins in SQL.

00:23.120 --> 00:27.840
So now let's say that we are joining the table A with the table B and we start from the table A.

00:27.960 --> 00:33.440
So it's going to take the columns and the rows of the table A and SQL are going to call it the left

00:33.480 --> 00:35.520
table because we started from there.

00:35.520 --> 00:40.680
And then we join it with the table B and SQL can call the second table as the right table.

00:40.680 --> 00:44.880
And here what's going to happen is we're going to take the columns and the rows from the right table

00:44.880 --> 00:51.560
and put it side by side with the columns and rows of the table a so we are like combining the columns.

00:51.560 --> 00:53.240
We are putting them side by side.

00:53.240 --> 00:55.480
And now if you say, you know what, I don't want to do that.

00:55.480 --> 00:59.280
I would like to combine the rows, both of the tables having the same columns.

00:59.280 --> 01:00.790
I just want to stack them.

01:00.790 --> 01:03.230
So we are now talking about another method.

01:03.230 --> 01:05.470
It is called the set operators.

01:05.470 --> 01:07.510
So here there is like no left and right.

01:07.510 --> 01:12.070
So since we started with the table A, the SQL going to take the columns and the rows of the table A

01:12.070 --> 01:16.070
and put it in the results, and then it's going to go to the second table, table B and it's going to

01:16.070 --> 01:20.270
take only the rows and put it below the rows of the the table a.

01:20.390 --> 01:24.790
So we are putting the rows beneath each others we are doing like appending.

01:24.790 --> 01:29.110
So that means as we are using the set operators, we are combining the rows.

01:29.150 --> 01:33.830
Our table is going to be longer, but with the joints we are combining the columns side by side and

01:33.830 --> 01:35.750
we are getting wider table.

01:35.790 --> 01:38.230
But now for each methods there are different types.

01:38.230 --> 01:42.230
So now for example, in order to do the joints, we have four very famous types.

01:42.230 --> 01:45.790
We can do an inner join full join, left join right join.

01:45.790 --> 01:47.190
But of course there are more than that.

01:47.190 --> 01:48.550
But those are the basics.

01:48.550 --> 01:54.590
And for the set methods we have as well types, we have the union, union all except and intersects.

01:54.590 --> 01:57.190
And for each methods there are like different rules.

01:57.190 --> 02:02.060
In order to join the tables, we have to define the key columns between the two tables.

02:02.060 --> 02:03.740
Don't worry, we're going to learn about that later.

02:03.740 --> 02:08.780
But this is the requirement in order to join tables and the requirement of combining tables using the

02:08.780 --> 02:14.300
set operators, the tables in your query should have the exact same number of columns.

02:14.340 --> 02:17.820
But here you don't need any like key in order to combine the tables.

02:17.820 --> 02:22.740
So guys, if you look at this in order to combine two tables, first you have to decide do I want to

02:22.740 --> 02:24.460
combine the columns or the rows.

02:24.460 --> 02:26.260
So first you have to decide the methods.

02:26.260 --> 02:31.020
And after that you have different types on how exactly you're going to go and combine the data.

02:31.020 --> 02:33.100
And of course there are rules that you have to follow.

02:33.140 --> 02:35.300
Now of course we're going to go and cover everything in the course.

02:35.340 --> 02:41.020
But now in this section we're going to learn how we're going to combine the tables using the SQL joins.

02:41.020 --> 02:43.500
So we're going to go and dive into this world.

02:47.340 --> 02:47.700
All right.

02:47.700 --> 02:49.700
So now what is exactly SQL joins.

02:49.740 --> 02:52.180
Now let's say that we have two tables on the left table.

02:52.180 --> 02:53.500
We have the customer name.

02:53.500 --> 02:54.780
So we have four customers.

02:54.780 --> 02:58.300
And on the right table we have the country information about the customer.

02:58.340 --> 03:03.210
And now we would like to query both of those informations, the names and the countries.

03:03.250 --> 03:08.370
Now in order to query those two tables in one query, first we have to connect them.

03:08.370 --> 03:13.850
And in order to connect those two tables we need a key, a column that exists on the left and on the

03:13.850 --> 03:14.610
right side.

03:14.610 --> 03:18.770
And by looking to this the common column here is the ID of the customer.

03:18.810 --> 03:24.330
Now once we connect those ideas together, we will be able to query those tables together.

03:24.330 --> 03:26.850
And SQL can start matching those IDs.

03:26.850 --> 03:31.570
So for the ID number one we will get the name Maria and the country Germany.

03:31.570 --> 03:34.770
And the ID two is connecting John to USA.

03:34.810 --> 03:37.650
And now you can see the ID three is not connectable.

03:37.650 --> 03:39.810
So we cannot connect it to the right side.

03:39.810 --> 03:44.010
But for the ID four we can use it in order to connect Martin to Germany.

03:44.010 --> 03:46.850
So this is exactly what happens if you join two tables.

03:46.850 --> 03:51.450
You connect those two tables using a common column, a key like the ID.

03:51.450 --> 03:55.610
And once we have matching value we can connect the two rows together.

03:55.610 --> 03:58.090
So this is what we mean with SQL joins.

04:02.000 --> 04:04.480
Now you might ask, why do we need actually joins?

04:04.520 --> 04:08.120
Well, the first and very important reason is to recombine your data.

04:08.120 --> 04:14.160
So now usually in databases the data about something like the customers could be spreaded into multiple

04:14.200 --> 04:14.840
tables.

04:14.840 --> 04:17.000
Like we could have a table called customers.

04:17.040 --> 04:22.440
Another one where we have the customer addresses and a third table where you can find the orders of

04:22.440 --> 04:27.160
the customers, and maybe another one where you can find the reviews of the customers.

04:27.160 --> 04:31.240
So as you can see, the data of the customers is spreaded into like four tables.

04:31.280 --> 04:35.960
Now how about I would like to see all the data about the customers in one results.

04:35.960 --> 04:39.800
So I would like to see the complete big picture about our customers.

04:39.800 --> 04:40.600
What we can do.

04:40.600 --> 04:45.240
We can go and connect those four tables using the SQL joins.

04:45.240 --> 04:51.480
And once we do that in one query, I will be able to combine all those tables in one big results.

04:51.480 --> 04:57.520
And this is the most important reason why we use SQL joins in order to combine all the data about a

04:57.520 --> 05:00.340
specific topic in order to see the big picture.

05:00.380 --> 05:04.740
Now, another reason why we use SQL joins is to do data enrichments.

05:04.740 --> 05:08.180
It is where I want to get an extra data and extra information.

05:08.180 --> 05:13.420
So let's say that you are querying the table customers and this is your main table the master table.

05:13.420 --> 05:15.860
So you are able to see all the data that you need.

05:15.860 --> 05:20.860
But sometimes what happens you would like to get an extra information from another table, like for

05:20.860 --> 05:23.100
example the zip codes of the countries.

05:23.100 --> 05:25.300
So you would like the help of another table.

05:25.300 --> 05:30.660
We call it a reference table or sometimes lookup table where there is like one extra information that

05:30.660 --> 05:34.580
you would like to add it to your master table to the primary source of your data.

05:34.580 --> 05:38.940
So now what we can do, we can join those two tables in order to enhance our table.

05:38.940 --> 05:42.820
So we are getting one extra relevant information for the customers.

05:42.820 --> 05:45.420
And this process we call it data enrichment.

05:45.420 --> 05:48.620
I'm getting an extra data for my main table.

05:48.620 --> 05:50.900
So this is another reason why we use joins.

05:50.940 --> 05:51.300
All right.

05:51.300 --> 05:55.900
So now so far we have used joins in order to get the data from two tables.

05:55.900 --> 05:58.770
But now there is another use case for the SQL joins.

05:58.770 --> 06:05.650
We use it in order to check the existence of your data in another table, or maybe as well the not existence.

06:05.650 --> 06:10.410
So let's say that I have a table called customers and I'm working with this table and doing queries.

06:10.410 --> 06:12.090
But now I would like to check something.

06:12.090 --> 06:15.730
I would like to check whether our customers did order something.

06:15.730 --> 06:21.210
Now in order to check that I need the help of another table, for example the table orders.

06:21.210 --> 06:25.090
So that means I'm using the table orders only for my check.

06:25.090 --> 06:28.890
So I don't want to get any extra data from the orders in my final results.

06:28.890 --> 06:32.650
I'm just using the table orders and we call them this table lookup.

06:32.650 --> 06:33.450
So now what we can do.

06:33.450 --> 06:35.610
We can connect those two tables together.

06:35.610 --> 06:41.570
And now based on the existence of the customers inside the second table, the orders, either the customer

06:41.610 --> 06:44.570
can stay in the final results or can be removed.

06:44.570 --> 06:47.850
So that means I'm filtering the data based on the join.

06:47.850 --> 06:52.730
And of course I can check as well the net existence I would like to see in the final results all the

06:52.730 --> 06:55.330
customers that didn't order anything.

06:55.330 --> 06:56.690
So it is the same scenario.

06:56.690 --> 07:01.200
So my friends, those are the main three reasons why we use SQL joins.

07:01.240 --> 07:05.560
First, if you want to combine the data from multiple tables in one big picture.

07:05.560 --> 07:09.120
So I use join in order to get the data from different tables.

07:09.120 --> 07:13.760
The second use case, you are working with one table, but you would like to get an extra information

07:13.760 --> 07:15.000
from another table.

07:15.000 --> 07:17.880
So you are doing like something called data enrichments.

07:17.880 --> 07:20.960
And in the third scenario, we don't want to combine the data.

07:20.960 --> 07:26.920
We want just to join it with another table in order to do a check, to check the existence of your records

07:26.920 --> 07:27.920
in another table.

07:27.920 --> 07:30.480
So this is why we need joins in SQL.

07:34.680 --> 07:39.160
Now there is like a lot of different possibilities on how to join tables, how to join the data.

07:39.200 --> 07:43.920
Now in order to make it easy to understand, we can a visual the tables as like two circles.

07:43.920 --> 07:46.320
So we have the table A and a table B.

07:46.360 --> 07:48.360
The table A is on the left side.

07:48.360 --> 07:49.960
We call it the left table.

07:49.960 --> 07:51.960
And the table B going to be on the right side.

07:51.960 --> 07:53.560
And we call it the right table.

07:53.560 --> 07:55.640
The side of the tables is very important.

07:55.680 --> 07:59.710
Now if you combine those two circles, you will get three different possibilities.

07:59.710 --> 08:01.150
The circles can overlap.

08:01.150 --> 08:05.630
And here exactly where we can have the matching data between the two tables.

08:05.630 --> 08:08.550
So the data is available on the left and on the right.

08:08.550 --> 08:12.590
Or another possibility you want to get all the data from one of the tables.

08:12.590 --> 08:15.150
So you can get all the rows from one circle.

08:15.150 --> 08:20.190
And the third possibility you want to get only the unmatching data from one table.

08:20.190 --> 08:25.830
So if something exists in one table but not in the other table, then we call it unmatching data.

08:25.830 --> 08:30.510
So those are the three scenarios that you have to ask yourself once you are combining tables.

08:30.510 --> 08:33.110
And this can generate a lot of joint types.

08:33.110 --> 08:35.430
So here we have like basic SQL joins.

08:35.630 --> 08:37.070
Those are the classical one.

08:37.110 --> 08:38.510
And here depends on the scenario.

08:38.510 --> 08:42.830
Whether you want only matching all other rows from either left or right.

08:42.830 --> 08:47.030
And we have advanced SQL joins where we focus on the Unmatching data.

08:47.070 --> 08:49.590
Now we're going to go and cover all those types one by one.

08:49.590 --> 08:51.430
So we're going to start first with the basics.

08:51.430 --> 08:55.950
And the first option that you have is to get all the data without joining tables.

08:55.950 --> 08:57.150
So let's see what this means.
